module lib/hf/boys
  The Boys function $F_n(x) = \int_0^1 t^{2n} e^{-x t^2} dt$, which every
  Gaussian integral with a $1/r$ in it reduces to: the attraction of an
  electron to a nucleus and the repulsion between two electrons. $n$ runs
  from zero to the total angular momentum of the integral, and $x$ grows with
  the exponents and the distance between the centers.

  Each member returns every order from $F_0$ to $F_{n_{\max}}$ at once,
  because an integral needs all of them and neighboring orders follow from
  one another by a recursion. The members use only `exp`, `sqrt`, `cos` and
  arithmetic, so the interpreter and the compiled C give the same bits.
  The production path is the series below a switch point and the large-$x$
  form above it; the quadrature member shares no code with the series and is
  there to check it. The tests hold every member against a table computed by
  mpmath (lib/hfref/boys).

boys_series_one  @e38fb5e5
  why   The one evaluation every other member starts from or is checked
        against. Every term of the series is positive, so no digits cancel.
  n       the order
  x       the argument
  term    the current term, $\frac{(2x)^k}{(2n+1)(2n+3)\cdots(2n+2k+1)}$
  total   the sum of the terms so far; $F_n(x)$ is $e^{-x}$ times it
  k       how many terms after the first have been added

boys_switch  @393ceaa9
  why   Below $x_n$ the large-$x$ form drops a tail that matters; above it the
        form is exact to rounding. The value is fixed by a test against the
        generator's table
        [test "each order's switch matches the generator's, and is right on both sides"].
  n     the highest order the caller needs
  xs    the switch for each order, as tools/boys_reference.py computes it

boys_down  @aaec5b5c
  why   Going down, $F_{n-1} = (2x F_n + e^{-x}) / (2n - 1)$ adds two positive
        numbers, so it keeps every digit; one evaluation at the top order,
        by the series or by the grid's Taylor sum, then gives all the others
        for the price of a multiply and an add.
  top   $F_{n_{\max}}(x)$
  nmax  the highest order wanted
  x     the argument
  out   $F_0 \ldots F_{n_{\max}}$
  ex    $e^{-x}$
  k     how many steps down have been taken
  n     the order just computed; the step writes order $n - 1$

boys_series_down  @4ab05d8d
  why   One series evaluation at the top order, then the downward step for
        the rest.
  nmax  the highest order wanted
  x     the argument

boys_asym_up  @b291afd1
  why   At large $x$ the series needs hundreds of terms, while
        $F_0 = \frac12\sqrt{\pi/x}$ is exact to rounding above the switch.
        Going up, $F_{n+1} = ((2n+1) F_n - e^{-x}) / (2x)$, subtracts, but
        above the switch $2x > 2n + 1$ and the subtraction loses no digits.
        Keeping the $e^{-x}$ term is what lets the switch sit near where the
        series gets slow instead of far above it.
  nmax  the highest order wanted
  x     the argument
  out   $F_0 \ldots F_{n_{\max}}$
  ex    $e^{-x}$

boys_values  @8eb35eae
  why   Callers name the production choice, not a member, so the choice can
        change in one place
        [test "boys_values matches mpmath to 1e-14 at every tabulated point and order"].
  nmax  the highest order wanted
  x     the argument

boys_gl_nodes  @31e0f0f2
  why   The quadrature member needs Gauss-Legendre nodes, and computing them
        here keeps the module free of a table of constants to check.
  m     the number of nodes
  GL    the result: nodes in row `0`, weights in row `1`
  z     the node being refined, starting from the cosine guess
  dp    $P_m'(z)$, the derivative of the Legendre polynomial, which Newton's
        step divides by and the weight formula uses
  p1    $P_j(z)$ as the three-term recurrence climbs; p2 and p3 are the two
        orders below it

boys_gl_pass  @5ee5111f
  why   One pass of the composite rule, for every order at once: the
        exponential is computed once per node and the powers $t^{2n}$ by
        repeated multiplication.
  nmax  the highest order wanted
  x     the argument
  npan  how many equal panels $[0, 1]$ is cut into
  GL    nodes and weights on $[-1, 1]$, from boys_gl_nodes
  out   $F_0 \ldots F_{n_{\max}}$
  width the width of one panel
  mid   the panel's midpoint
  t     the node mapped into the panel
  w     the node's weight times $e^{-x t^2}$, times $t^{2n}$ as $n$ climbs

boys_quadrature  @1916339c
  why   The only member that shares no formula with the series, so it is the
        one that can catch a mistake in it.
  nmax    the highest order wanted
  x       the argument
  GL      the Gauss-Legendre nodes and weights
  prev    the previous pass's values
  cur     this pass's values
  npan    the panel count, doubled each pass
  change  the largest relative change of any order between the two passes

boys_grid_range  @45a29d20
  why   Integral codes evaluate the Boys function once per primitive pair or quartet, at nearby
        arguments; a table at evenly spaced nodes turns each evaluation into
        a short Taylor sum. The extra `BOYS_TAYLOR_K` orders per node are the
        derivatives that sum needs, since $dF_n/dx = -F_{n+1}$.
  ncap  the highest order the grid serves
  i0    the index of the first node
  npts  how many nodes
  ncol  the columns of a row: the header, or the orders up to `ncap + BOYS_TAYLOR_K`
  G     the grid: header row, then one row of orders per node, from the series

boys_grid_new  @09c1dab7
  why   A caller builds one grid inside its own integral def, so the grid is
        freed when that def returns and nothing global holds it.
  nmax  the highest order the caller needs
  xmax  the largest argument the caller expects
  top   where the grid stops: `xmax`, or the switch when that comes first

boys_grid_eval  @7928c3ea
  why   The fast path of the integral codes. Anything the grid does not cover
        goes to the series, so a grid built too small costs time and not
        accuracy [test "a grid built once by the caller matches mpmath to 1e-13"].
  G      the grid from boys_grid_new
  nmax   the highest order wanted
  x      the argument
  i      the index of the node nearest `x`
  r      that node's row in `G`
  d      $x - x_0$, the distance from the node
  fac    $(-d)^k / k!$, the Taylor factor of term `k`
  total  $F_{n_{\max}}(x)$ as the Taylor sum builds it; boys_down gives the lower orders

boys_taylor  @c0f5e4bf
  why   Lets the registry's tests run the grid's evaluation code on the same
        footing as the other members.
  nmax  the highest order wanted
  x     the argument
  G     a one-node grid at the node nearest `x`

boys_methods  @4b3e541a
  why   The tests loop over the members by index, and a program can pick one by
        an option, without either naming the defs.
